From 35a1567525d86d3e493c5a80cc471546e26443b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Pooya Parsa <pooya@pi0.io>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:43:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: park every task and resume it on a fresh stack, and
 snapshots

A sleeping task's continuation lives on its Wasm stack, which the host
engine keeps (JSPI) and nothing can save. Add parking: every task stops
at a point where its whole state is in guest memory, and each resumes on
a fresh stack. That is what a snapshot of the machine needs, and the
resume path is exercised on every cycle, with no snapshot taken.

kwparkd runs a cycle on request (a write to /proc/kwpark):

 - User tasks get TIF_NOTIFY_SIGNAL, which ends interruptible sleeps; the
   syscall restarts or, for the few that do not restart after a stop
   either (epoll_wait, socket timeouts), returns -EINTR. The task parks at
   the top of user_mode_loop(), after the exit work, in TASK_IDLE, so
   signals do not wake it.
 - Then kernel threads are woken if they sleep interruptibly or idle, and
   park at their kw_park_point() (linux/kw_park.h).
 - kwparkd parks last. The idle loop then sees kw_park_final and, with
   interrupts off and nothing runnable, checks that every task is parked,
   a zombie or not yet started: the consistent point. It marks every
   parked task KW_PARK_RESUMING and TIF_NEVER_RUN and wakes it, so the
   next switch starts it through ret_from_fork, which calls
   kw_park_resume(): finish the switch as schedule() would (without
   storing the child TID again), then fn(arg) for a kernel thread, or the
   interrupt exit work and user_mode_loop() for a user task. The host
   drops the old stack.

A task that does not park within two seconds (stopped, traced, in a
D-state sleep, a kernel thread without a park point) ends the cycle with
-EBUSY, naming it, after resuming everything.

Snapshots: the host raises WASM_IRQ_SNAPSHOT for a cycle and gets the new
import wasm_snapshot_point() at its end; on success the call is made at
the consistent point, and the host copies the memory during it. A
restored snapshot enters at the new export kw_resume instead of _start,
as the idle task on a fresh stack: __kw_resume() calls kw_host_reset() so
that kwemu forgets the JIT slots of the old instance, and resumes kwparkd
alone, which steps the realtime clock to the host's, reseeds the RNG with
add_vmfork_randomness() and then resumes every other task.
---
